const beforeForm = "<blockquote class=\"mb-12 rounded-lg p-5 bg-[#E1F5EE] border-l-4 border-[#1D9E75]\">\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>TL;DR</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>A faucet that spits air once after plumbing work, a power outage, or a new pump is usually trapped air. Run the faucets and it clears.</li>\n<li>Sputtering that keeps coming back means air is getting into the system. When it happens and which faucets do it tell you why.</li>\n<li><strong>Sputters at the end of long water use:</strong> the well may be running low. <strong>Sputters on the first draw after the water sits:</strong> suspect a leaking check valve or drop pipe. <strong>Only hot faucets:</strong> it&#39;s the water heater.</li>\n<li><strong>Fizzy, milky water that clears from the bottom up, or a hissing wellhead:</strong> dissolved gas, possibly methane. Methane can build up in enclosed spaces and is explosive, so test for it.</li>\n<li>If the pump runs while the faucets spit air, turn it off. A pump pulling air can overheat and burn out.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Air in the water lines is one of the most common complaints on a private well, and one of the most confusing, because the same sputter can come from something harmless or from a pump that&#39;s about to fail. The good news is that two questions narrow it down fast: <strong>when</strong> does it sputter, and <strong>which faucets</strong> do it.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">This page walks through every common cause, from trapped air you can clear in five minutes to problems that need a well contractor. If your well is running low, or your pump is failing, we cover those in depth in <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-running-dry\">well running dry</a> and <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-pump-failing\">well pump failing</a>. This guide helps you figure out which one you&#39;re dealing with.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Safety First</h2>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>If the pump is running and the faucets are spitting air, turn off the pump&#39;s breaker.</strong> A submersible pump pulling air has no water to cool it and can burn out quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>If you smell gas, hear hissing at the wellhead, or the water fizzes like soda,</strong> don&#39;t light anything near the well, the well house, or the basement where the water line enters. Ventilate enclosed spaces and see the gas section below.</li>\n<li><strong>If you haven&#39;t used the hot water in two weeks or more,</strong> open a hot faucet in the kitchen or bathroom and let it run for a few minutes before using any appliance, with no flames or sparks nearby. Water heaters can build up hydrogen gas when unused.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">The When-and-Where Rule</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Two questions point to the cause.</p>\n<div class=\"w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[760px] text-sm border-collapse\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#E1F5EE]\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">When does it sputter?</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Which faucets?</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Most likely cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Once, right after plumbing work, a power outage, a new pump, or shocking the well</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Trapped air. Harmless.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">At the end of long water use, like laundry plus showers, or after a lawn sprinkler</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Well running low. Water drops below the pump intake.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">On the first draw after the water has sat for a while, like first thing in the morning</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Leaking check valve, drop pipe, or pitless adapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Every time the pump starts</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Leaking check valve or drop pipe, or a failing pressure tank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Constantly, with milky or fizzy water that clears from the bottom of the glass</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Dissolved gas in the water: methane, carbon dioxide, or hydrogen sulfide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Only from the hot side</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Hot only</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Water heater</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Only one faucet</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">One</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Aerator or the plumbing to that fixture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Jet pump system, sputtering steadily</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Suction leak or failing foot valve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Older galvanized pressure tank, steady spurts of air</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Air volume control or snifter valve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Constant-pressure system, sputtering with pressure dips or a fault code on the controller</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Low water or a drive protection setting. See the constant-pressure section.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Shallow driven or sand point well, worse in late summer</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">All</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Water table dropping toward the well point</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">5-Minute Diagnostic</h2>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Note the timing.</strong> First draw of the day? End of a long shower? Every time the pump kicks on? Write it down.</li>\n<li><strong>Test hot and cold separately.</strong> Run only cold at a faucet, then only hot. If only hot sputters, skip to the water heater section.</li>\n<li><strong>Do the glass test.</strong> Fill a clear glass and set it down. Milky water that clears from the bottom up within a minute or two is air or gas bubbles. Cloudiness that settles to the bottom as sediment is something else. See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/brown-rusty-well-water\">brown or rusty well water guide</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch the pressure gauge</strong> near the pressure tank while someone runs water. Does the pressure drop to zero, flutter, or drop much lower than the pump&#39;s cut-in setting? Does the pump run long without building pressure?</li>\n<li><strong>Listen at the wellhead and pressure tank.</strong> Hissing at the well cap can mean gas. Gurgling at the tank or a pump that clicks on and off rapidly points to air or low water.</li>\n<li><strong>Think back.</strong> Any recent plumbing work, a power outage, a new pump, shock chlorination, a dry spell, or new heavy water use like a sprinkler or a pool fill?</li>\n<li><strong>Check what kind of system you have.</strong> A submersible pump with a bladder tank, a jet pump, or an older galvanized tank. Our guide to <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/guides/how-well-pump-works\">how a well pump works</a> helps if you&#39;re not sure.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Now match what you found to the causes below.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 1: Trapped Air After Work on the System</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Signs:</strong> sputtering started right after plumbing work, a pump replacement, a pressure tank swap, a long power outage, or shocking the well, and it gets better each time you run water.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">When the system is drained or opened, air gets in. It collects at high points in the pipes and comes out in bursts the first few times you run water.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>How to fix it:</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Open the cold faucet closest to the pressure tank and let it run until the flow is steady with no spitting.</li>\n<li>Move to the next faucet, working outward toward the farthest one in the house, and do the same at each.</li>\n<li>Do the hot faucets last, one at a time.</li>\n<li>Don&#39;t forget showers, the laundry hookup, and outdoor spigots in warm weather.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">It should clear within a day or two of normal use. If it doesn&#39;t, or comes back later, air is getting in from somewhere, so keep going.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 2: The Well Is Running Low</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Signs:</strong> water is fine for short uses but sputters at the end of a long run, like back-to-back showers, laundry, or watering the garden. It&#39;s worse in dry weather, late summer, or a drought. The pump may run longer than usual or cycle rapidly near the end.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">The pump sits at a set depth in the well. When you draw water faster than the well can refill, the water level drops toward the pump&#39;s intake. Once it gets close, the pump pulls a mix of water and air, and the faucets spit.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>What to do now:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Stop using water for a few hours to let the well recover.</li>\n<li>Spread out heavy water use: laundry on one day, showers at different times, no lawn watering.</li>\n<li>Turn off the pump if it&#39;s running without moving water.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">This is an early warning sign. Our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-running-dry\">well running dry guide</a> covers the stages, how to measure your well&#39;s recovery, and the long-term fixes, from adding storage to lowering the pump.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Related:</strong> a pump that&#39;s too large for the well can draw it down fast even when the well itself is fine. That&#39;s worth asking a contractor about if your well is newer or the pump was recently replaced.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 3: Leaking Check Valve, Drop Pipe, or Pitless Adapter</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Signs:</strong> the first draw after water has sat, like first thing in the morning, spits air, then the water runs normally. The pump may run longer than usual to build pressure. Some homes also notice the pump kicking on briefly when no one is using water.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">The drop pipe carries water from the pump up to the surface. One or more check valves keep the water in that pipe from draining back down the well when the pump shuts off. When a check valve leaks, or the drop pipe or the pitless adapter (the fitting where the pipe exits the well casing underground) develops a leak, water slowly drains back down the well. Air fills the pipe in its place. The next time you open a faucet, that air comes out first.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>How to check:</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Note the pressure on the gauge at night with no water in use.</li>\n<li>Check it again in the morning before anyone uses water.</li>\n<li>If the pressure has dropped noticeably and you have no leaks in the house, water is leaking back somewhere. A leak in the house would also show as a dropping gauge, so check toilets and faucets first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>The fix:</strong> check valves, drop pipes, and pitless adapters are down the well or underground. A well contractor pulls the pump to inspect and replace them, and often recommends replacing the check valve any time the pump is out. See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-pump-failing\">well pump failing guide</a> for what a pump pull involves and our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/cost-guides/well-pump-replacement-cost\">well pump replacement cost guide</a> for typical costs.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 4: Pressure Tank Problems</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Signs:</strong> sputtering along with rapid pump cycling, pressure that swings up and down quickly, or water spurting at faucets every time the pump starts.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Bladder or diaphragm tanks.</strong> Most modern tanks keep air and water separate with a rubber bladder. If the bladder fails, air and water can mix, and the tank stops cushioning pressure. The pump short-cycles, and some homes see spurting. To check, turn off the pump, drain the system, and press the pin in the tank&#39;s air valve. If water comes out instead of air, the bladder has failed and the tank needs replacing.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Galvanized tanks.</strong> Older tanks with no bladder rely on a device called an air volume control (AVC) to keep the right amount of air in the tank. On some systems, a small snifter valve on the pump line lets air in on each cycle. If either one sticks or fails, the tank can end up with too much air, and the extra air gets pushed out through the faucets.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">For both types, our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/guides/well-pressure-tank-guide\">pressure tank guide</a> explains how each one works, how to test it, and when to replace it. Our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/cost-guides/pressure-tank-replacement-cost\">pressure tank replacement cost guide</a> covers the cost. Many contractors recommend replacing a galvanized tank with a bladder tank rather than repairing it.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>A quick gauge check:</strong> a working system shows the pressure rising smoothly while the pump runs and dropping smoothly as you use water. Swings of 10 to 20 PSI every few seconds suggest a tank problem. A reliable <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B08V4JgZl\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">0 to 100 PSI pressure gauge</a> makes this much easier to read, and gauges themselves wear out.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 5: Suction Leak on a Jet Pump</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Signs:</strong> you have a jet pump, either an above-ground pump on a shallow well or a convertible jet pump on a deeper one. The water sputters steadily, the pump struggles to build pressure, or it loses prime.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Jet pumps pull water up by suction. Any leak on the suction side of the pump lets air in, and the pump sends that air to your faucets. Common spots:</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Loose or cracked fittings on the suction line between the well and the pump</li>\n<li>A worn gasket or seal on the pump body</li>\n<li>A failing <strong>foot valve</strong>, the one-way valve at the bottom of the suction pipe in the well, which lets water drain back down</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>How to check:</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>With the pump running, listen and feel along the suction line and fittings for hissing or sucking.</li>\n<li>Look for wet spots or mineral crust around fittings, which mark slow leaks.</li>\n<li>If the pump loses prime after sitting, the foot valve or a check valve is likely leaking.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>The fix:</strong> tighten or replace leaking fittings with fresh thread sealant. A failed <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B0fKmquN0\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">foot valve</a> has to be pulled up and replaced. After any repair, the pump will need to be primed again. See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/how-to-prime-a-well-pump\">how to prime a well pump guide</a>.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 6: Gas in the Water</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Signs:</strong> water comes out milky or fizzy, like soda, and clears from the bottom of the glass up. Sputtering is steady, not tied to timing. You may hear hissing at the well cap. Some wells also smell like rotten eggs.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Groundwater can carry dissolved gases. Under pressure in the well, they stay dissolved. When the water reaches your faucet and the pressure drops, they bubble out, the same way a soda fizzes when you open it.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>The common gases:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Carbon dioxide:</strong> harmless to drink, common in some areas, and often comes with acidic water.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrogen sulfide:</strong> the rotten egg smell. See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-water-smells-like-sulfur\">sulfur smell guide</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Methane:</strong> colorless and odorless. It isn&#39;t considered toxic to drink, but it&#39;s flammable. If it collects in a well house, a well pit, or a closed basement, it can reach explosive levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>What to do:</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Don&#39;t smoke or use open flames near the well, well house, or where the water line enters the house.</li>\n<li>Ventilate any enclosed space around the wellhead or water equipment.</li>\n<li>Have the water tested for dissolved methane. Many state health departments and certified labs offer it. A standard bacteria and minerals test won&#39;t detect it. See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/cost-guides/well-water-testing-cost\">well water testing cost guide</a>.</li>\n<li>If methane is present, a well contractor can vent the well cap to the outside and install equipment that strips gas from the water before it enters the house.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Gas in the water is less common than the mechanical causes above, but it&#39;s the one to rule out first if the water fizzes or the wellhead hisses.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 7: Only the Hot Water Sputters</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Signs:</strong> cold water runs smoothly, but hot faucets spit and pop.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">That narrows it to the water heater. Heating water drives some dissolved gas out of solution, which can collect at the top of the tank and come out in bursts. A water heater that&#39;s been sitting unused can also build up hydrogen gas from a reaction between the water and the tank&#39;s anode rod, especially with well water.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>What to do:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Run the hot water at a sink for a few minutes to clear it. If the heater sat unused for two weeks or more, do this with no flames or sparks nearby, as noted in the safety section.</li>\n<li>If it keeps happening, have the water heater checked. Flushing it and replacing the anode rod, sometimes with a different type suited to well water, usually solves it.</li>\n<li>If the hot water also smells like rotten eggs, see our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-water-smells-like-sulfur\">sulfur smell guide</a>, since the anode rod is a common cause there too.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cause 8: Only One Faucet Sputters</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">If only one faucet does it, the problem is almost certainly at that fixture.</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Unscrew the aerator</strong> at the tip of the faucet and clean out any sediment or mineral buildup.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the shutoff valves</strong> under that sink. A partly closed or failing valve can cause spitting.</li>\n<li><strong>If it&#39;s a single-handle faucet,</strong> a worn cartridge can cause sputtering. Replacing it is a common DIY repair.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Special Cases: Constant-Pressure Systems and Driven Wells</h2>\n<h3 class=\"text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3 mt-8\">Constant-Pressure Systems</h3>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Some newer wells use a constant-pressure system. A controller, often called a variable frequency drive, speeds the pump up and down to hold steady pressure, and these systems use a much smaller pressure tank. A few things are different:</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Most controllers have dry-run protection.</strong> If the pump starts pulling air because the well is low, the controller shuts it down and displays a fault code or blinking light. Sputtering followed by no water and a fault on the controller usually means low water, not a broken controller. Check the controller&#39;s manual for what the code means and how long it waits before restarting.</li>\n<li><strong>The small tank still matters.</strong> A tank with the wrong air charge can make pressure dip and surge, which some people describe as sputtering. The air charge setting for these tanks is often different from a standard system&#39;s, so follow the controller manual.</li>\n<li><strong>Don&#39;t reset the controller over and over.</strong> Repeated restarts on a low well pull air through the pump each time. Let the well recover first.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3 mt-8\">Driven Wells and Sand Points</h3>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">A driven well, often called a sand point or well point, is a narrow pipe pounded into shallow ground with a screened tip at the bottom. They&#39;re common at cottages, cabins, and older rural homes, and they almost always use an above-ground jet pump.</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>The water table matters more.</strong> Because the well point sits only a short way below the water table, a dry summer can drop the water close to the screen. The pump starts pulling air, and the faucets sputter, often in late summer and early fall.</li>\n<li><strong>The screen can clog.</strong> Over years, sediment and mineral buildup on the screen slow the water coming in, and the pump pulls air even when the water table is normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaks are common at the joints.</strong> Driven wells are made of short pipe sections threaded together. A loose joint above the water lets air in, just like a jet pump suction leak.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>What helps:</strong> use less water during dry spells, check the suction-side joints you can reach, and talk to a well contractor about cleaning or replacing the point, or driving it deeper if the water table has dropped for good.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Three Common Scenarios</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Here&#39;s how the diagnosis plays out in a few situations well owners run into often.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Scenario 1: Every morning, the first faucet spits air for a few seconds, then runs fine.</strong>\nThe overnight gauge test shows pressure falling from 60 PSI to about 45 PSI with no water used and no leaks in the house. Water is draining back down the well overnight, so the most likely cause is a leaking check valve on the drop pipe. Repair means a pump pull, and a contractor will check the drop pipe and pitless adapter while the pump is out.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Scenario 2: Late August, the washing machine is on its third load and the shower starts sputtering.</strong>\nEarlier in the summer, the same routine caused no problems. The well is falling behind on heavy days during a dry stretch. Spreading laundry across the week stops it. If it happens every summer, the long-term fixes in our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-running-dry\">well running dry guide</a> are worth pricing.</p>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Scenario 3: Water is milky in a glass, clears from the bottom up in about a minute, and there&#39;s a faint hiss at the well cap.</strong>\nNothing mechanical explains this. It&#39;s dissolved gas coming out of the water. Because the wellhead hisses, a methane test is the next step, and until the results come back, keep flames and sparks away from the well cap and any enclosed space where the water line enters.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Keep a Sputter Log</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">If the cause isn&#39;t obvious, track it for a week. It takes a minute a day, and it gives a well contractor what they need to find the problem on the first visit.</p>\n<div class=\"sputter-log w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[760px] text-sm border-collapse\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#E1F5EE]\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Date and time</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Which faucet</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Hot, cold, or both</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">What you were doing</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Pressure on the gauge</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Weather in the last few days</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n<td class=\"h-12 p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Patterns jump out fast. Sputtering only in the morning points to drainback. Only on heavy-use days points to the well. Every time, with fizzy water, points to gas.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">What to Expect When a Contractor Comes Out</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Knowing the process helps you understand the quote.</p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Questions and your log.</strong> They&#39;ll ask when it happens and what you&#39;ve noticed. Bring your sputter log.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure tank check.</strong> They&#39;ll check the tank&#39;s air charge and look for a failed bladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure and run-time test.</strong> They&#39;ll watch the gauge while the pump runs and may check whether pressure holds overnight.</li>\n<li><strong>Water level and yield.</strong> On a well with access, they may measure the static water level and how far it drops while pumping. This tells them whether the well is running low.</li>\n<li><strong>Pump pull, if needed.</strong> If they suspect the check valve, drop pipe, or pump, they&#39;ll pull it up to inspect it. This is the expensive step, so ask what they found that points to it before agreeing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>Questions to ask:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>What makes you think the problem is down the well rather than at the tank?</li>\n<li>If you pull the pump, will you replace the check valve while it&#39;s out?</li>\n<li>Is the pump set at the right depth for this well?</li>\n<li>Is the pump sized correctly for the well&#39;s yield?</li>\n<li>Do you test for dissolved gas, or should I use a lab?</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">When Sputtering Means Act Now</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Some situations can&#39;t wait:</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>The pump runs nonstop but the faucets spit air or nothing comes out.</strong> Turn off the breaker. See <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/no-water-from-well\">no water from well</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The pump clicks on and off every few seconds.</strong> That&#39;s rapid short cycling and wears out the pump fast. See <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-pump-short-cycling\">well pump short cycling</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>You smell gas or hear hissing at the wellhead.</strong> Ventilate and test for methane.</li>\n<li><strong>The water turns muddy or sandy along with the sputtering.</strong> The pump may be pulling from the bottom of the well, or a well screen may be failing. Limit water use and call a contractor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Cost Breakdown and 10-Year Total Cost of Ownership</h2>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">Typical estimates. Costs vary widely by well depth and region.</p>\n<div class=\"w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[760px] text-sm border-collapse\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#E1F5EE]\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Cause and fix</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Typical cost</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900 border border-slate-200\">Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Trapped air, bleed the lines</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">$0</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Clears in a day or two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Low well, change water use</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">$0</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Ongoing habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Low well, add storage or lower the pump</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Varies widely</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/problems/well-running-dry\">well running dry guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Replace a check valve during a pump pull</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Often several hundred dollars, mostly labor</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Usually done along with other pump work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Replace a drop pipe or pitless adapter</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Varies with depth</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Often done with a pump pull</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Replace the pressure tank</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/cost-guides/pressure-tank-replacement-cost\">pressure tank replacement cost guide</a></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Bladder tanks replace galvanized ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Fix a jet pump suction leak</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">$0 to $50 DIY</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">More if a foot valve has to be pulled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Methane test</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Varies by lab</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Worth it if water fizzes or the wellhead hisses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Methane venting or removal</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Varies by system</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Contractor job</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Water heater flush or anode rod</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">DIY or a service call</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Fixes most hot-only sputtering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">Replace a pump burned out by running on air</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">See our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/cost-guides/well-pump-replacement-cost\">well pump replacement cost guide</a></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700\">The expensive outcome to avoid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\"><strong>The 10-year view:</strong> most air problems cost little or nothing to fix if caught early. The costly outcome is a submersible pump that burns out from repeatedly pulling air, which is why sputtering is worth diagnosing instead of living with.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">Common Mistakes</h2>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Ignoring sputtering that keeps coming back.</strong> Trapped air clears on its own. Recurring air means something is letting it in.</li>\n<li><strong>Letting the pump run while faucets spit air.</strong> A submersible pump pulling air can burn out.</li>\n<li><strong>Assuming milky water is a contaminant.</strong> If it clears from the bottom up, it&#39;s bubbles. Test for gas if it&#39;s fizzy or the wellhead hisses.</li>\n<li><strong>Lighting a flame near a hissing wellhead.</strong> Methane is flammable.</li>\n<li><strong>Watering the lawn on a low-yield well.</strong> It&#39;s the fastest way to draw the water below the pump.</li>\n<li><strong>Replacing the pump without checking the check valve and drop pipe.</strong> A leak there will keep causing air even with a new pump.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjusting the pressure switch to fix sputtering.</strong> It rarely helps and can make low-water problems worse. Our <a class=\"text-[#1D9E75] underline hover:text-[#0F6E56]\" href=\"/guides/well-pressure-switch\">pressure switch guide</a> covers when adjustment makes sense.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 mt-12\">When to Call a Pro</h2>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Sputtering keeps coming back after you&#39;ve bled the lines</li>\n<li>Your pressure gauge drops overnight with no water in use</li>\n<li>The pressure tank&#39;s air valve spits water when pressed</li>\n<li>The pump runs without building pressure, or loses prime repeatedly</li>\n<li>The water fizzes, the wellhead hisses, or you suspect methane</li>\n<li>The water turns sandy or muddy along with the sputtering</li>\n<li>Your well runs low regularly and you need more storage or a pump adjustment</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4\">DIY-friendly: bleeding air from the lines, adjusting water use, the glass test, cleaning aerators, checking a jet pump&#39;s fittings, and flushing the water heater.</p>\n"
const glossary = "<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Drop pipe:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">The pipe that carries water from a submersible pump up to the surface.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Check valve:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">A one-way valve that keeps water in the drop pipe from draining back into the well.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Pitless adapter:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">The underground fitting where the water line exits the well casing below the frost line.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Foot valve:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">A one-way valve at the bottom of a jet pump's suction pipe that keeps the pump primed.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Bladder tank:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">A pressure tank with a rubber bladder that keeps air and water separate.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Galvanized tank:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">An older pressure tank with no bladder, where air sits directly on top of the water.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Air volume control (AVC):</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">A device on galvanized tanks that keeps the right amount of air in the tank.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Snifter valve:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">A small valve on some systems that lets air into the line on each pump cycle to recharge a galvanized tank.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Drawdown:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">How far the water level in the well drops while the pump is running.</dd></div>\n<div class=\"bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5\"><dt class=\"font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2\">Dissolved gas:</dt><dd class=\"text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed\">Gas such as methane, carbon dioxide, or hydrogen sulfide held in groundwater under pressure.</dd></div>"
const related = "<a href=\"/problems/well-running-dry\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">Well Running Dry</a>\n<a href=\"/problems/well-pump-failing\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">Well Pump Failing</a>\n<a href=\"/guides/well-pressure-tank-guide\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">Well Pressure Tank Guide</a>\n<a href=\"/problems/well-pump-short-cycling\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">Well Pump Short Cycling</a>\n<a href=\"/problems/no-water-from-well\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">No Water From Well</a>\n<a href=\"/problems/how-to-prime-a-well-pump\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">How to Prime a Well Pump</a>\n<a href=\"/problems/well-water-smells-like-sulfur\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">Well Water Smells Like Sulfur</a>\n<a href=\"/guides/how-well-pump-works\" class=\"block bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-[#1D9E75]\">How a Well Pump Works</a>"

